00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.140 align:start position:0%
 
I  you  spotted wing Drosophila is a small  vinegar fly native to Japan it was first  seen in the United States when it was  noted in Hawaii in the 1980s the fly  reached the mainland in summer of two  thousand eight when it was brought to  central California during this first  year spotted wing caused an estimated  500 billion dollars in damages with some  farmers recording crop losses of up to  eighty percent the fly traveled across  the rest of the country and is now  widespread throughout the US and has  also been seen in parts of Europe  Australia New Zealand have imposed  quarantine restrictions on Washington  State cherries but as of yet no other  preventative measures are being taken to  guard against further spread of this  insect spotted wing was first seen New  York in the fall of 2011 but because the  fly is relatively new to the Northeast  their damage has been difficult to  quantify however since we are now aware  of the threat that the fly poses their  populations are being monitored spotted  wing is very similar to the fruit flies  that we are all familiar with however  these slides have a serrated ovipositor  allowing the females to lay eggs and  under ripe fruit unlike other disciple  of species which can only lay eggs in  overripe or rotting fruit females can  lay up to 16 eggs in one day and over  300 eggs in their lifetime which can be  up to nine weeks long studies have shown  that up to 13 generations of flies can  be produced per year and adults are  capable of overwintering a respiratory  horn also called a sting shown here  indicates the opposition site of the egg  eggs hatch within three days and larval  feeding immediately begins adulthood can  be reached after only eight days larval  feeding causes the collapse of fruit  tissue leaving the tell-tale  indentations and berries indicating that  the maggots are present this damage to  the skin of the fruit also makes it  vulnerable to bacterial and fungal and  section when further contains these eggs  is harvested within two days the maggots  will emerge making the product  completely unmarketable right now the  only way to control spotted wing is to  the use of full coverage sprays directed  towards controlling adult populations  there is currently nothing that can be  done to salvage fruit that already  contained developing eggs sprays must be  applied weekly and it is encouraged to  use a variety of insecticides with  different modes of action as a way of  preventing resistance however this is  not the ideal way to control a pest as  pesticide application here is based on a  time schedule as opposed to an IPM  approach this results in heavy spraying  which is both costly for farmers and bad  for the environment farmers are also  encouraged to closely monitor their  fruit and harvest it immediately once it  reaches ripeness and also to dispose of  infested fruit  the good news is that male flies become  sterile a 30 degree in that new york  state's natural cold temperatures may  help limit their population growth and  protect our fruit as scientists continue  to study spotted wing Drosophila and  make discovery such as this we will be  able to develop a better pest management  strategy for the future

